{"timestamp":"2026-09-20T06:03:24.557Z","ordinal":72,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a0bd69-860d-7873-b535-9b1c761fbf22","role":"developer","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"## Memory\n\nYou have access to a memory folder with guidance from prior runs. It can save\ntime and help you stay consistent. Use it whenever it is likely to help.\n\nDecision boundary: should you use memory for a new user query?\n\n- Skip memory ONLY when the request is clearly self-contained and does not need\n  workspace history, conventions, or prior decisions.\n- Hard skip examples: current time/date, simple translation, simple sentence\n  rewrite, one-line shell command, trivial formatting.\n- Use memory by default when ANY of these are true:\n  - the query mentions workspace/repo/module/path/files in MEMORY_SUMMARY below,\n  - the user asks for prior context / consistency / previous decisions,\n  - the task is ambiguous and could depend on earlier project choices,\n  - the ask is a non-trivial and related to MEMORY_SUMMARY below.\n- If unsure, do a quick memory pass.\n\nMemory layout (general -> specific):\n\n- /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/memory_summary.md (already provided below; do NOT open again)\n- /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md (searchable registry; primary file to query)\n- /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/skills/<skill-name>/ (skill folder)\n  - SKILL.md (entrypoint instructions)\n  - scripts/ (optional helper scripts)\n  - examples/ (optional example outputs)\n  - templates/ (optional templates)\n- /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ (per-rollout recaps + evidence snippets)\n  - The paths of these entries can be found in /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md or /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ as `rollout_path`\n  - These files are append-only `jsonl`: `session_meta.payload.id` identifies the session, `turn_context` marks turn boundaries, `event_msg` is the lightweight status stream, and `response_item` contains actual messages, tool calls, and tool outputs.\n  - For efficient lookup, prefer matching the filename suffix or `session_meta.payload.id`; avoid broad full-content scans unless needed.\n\nQuick memory pass (when applicable):\n\n1. Skim the MEMORY_SUMMARY below and extract task-relevant keywords.\n2. Search /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md using those keywords.\n3. Only if MEMORY.md directly points to rollout summaries/skills, open the 1-2\n   most relevant files under /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ or\n   /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/skills/.\n4. If above are not clear and you need exact commands, error text, or precise evidence, search over `rollout_path` for more evidence.\n5. If there are no relevant hits, stop memory lookup and continue normally.\n\nQuick-pass budget:\n\n- Keep memory lookup lightweight: ideally <= 4-6 search steps before main work.\n- Avoid broad scans of all rollout summaries.\n\nDuring execution: if you hit repeated errors, confusing behavior, or suspect\nrelevant prior context, redo the quick memory pass.\n\nHow to decide whether to verify memory:\n\n- Consider both risk of drift and verification effort.\n- If a fact is likely to drift and is cheap to verify, verify it before\n  answering.\n- If a fact is likely to drift but verification is expensive, slow, or\n  disruptive, it is acceptable to answer from memory in an interactive turn,\n  but you should say that it is memory-derived, note that it may be stale, and\n  consider offering to refresh it live.\n- If a fact is lower-drift and expensive to verify, it is usually fine to\n  answer from memory directly.\n\nWhen answering from memory without current verification:\n\n- If you rely on memory for a fact that you did not verify in the current turn,\n  say so briefly in the final answer.\n- If that fact is plausibly drift-prone or comes from an older note, older\n  snapshot, or prior run summary, say that it may be stale or outdated.\n- If live verification was skipped and a refresh would be useful in the\n  interactive context, consider offering to verify or refresh it live.\n- Do not present unverified memory-derived facts as confirmed-current.\n- Prefer a short refresh offer for interactive questions, especially about prior\n  results, commands, timing, or older snapshots.\n\nMemory citation requirements:\n\n- If ANY relevant memory files were used: append exactly one\n`<oai-mem-citation>` block as the VERY LAST content of the final reply.\n  Normal responses should include the answer first, then append the\n`<oai-mem-citation>` block at the end.\n- Use this exact structure for programmatic parsing:\n```\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:234-236|note=[responsesapi citation extraction code pointer]\nrollout_summaries/2026-02-17T21-23-02-LN3m-example.md:10-12|note=[weekly report format]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043\n019c7714-3b77-74d1-9866-e1f484aae2ab\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>\n```\n- `citation_entries` is for rendering:\n  - one citation entry per line\n  - format: `<file>:<line_start>-<line_end>|note=[<how memory was used>]`\n  - use file paths relative to the memory base path (for example, `MEMORY.md`,\n    `rollout_summaries/...`, `skills/...`)\n  - only cite files actually used under the memory base path (do not cite\n    workspace files as memory citations)\n  - if you used `MEMORY.md` and then a rollout summary/skill file, cite both\n  - list entries in order of importance (most important first)\n  - `note` should be short, single-line, and use simple characters only (avoid\n    unusual symbols, no newlines)\n- `rollout_ids` is for us to track what previous rollouts you find useful:\n  - include one rollout id per line\n  - rollout ids should look like UUIDs (for example,\n    `019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043`)\n  - include unique ids only; do not repeat ids\n  - an empty `<rollout_ids>` section is allowed if no rollout ids are available\n  - you can find rollout ids in rollout summary files and MEMORY.md\n  - do not include file paths or notes in this section\n  - For every `citation_entries`, try to find and cite the corresponding rollout id if possible\n- Never include memory citations inside pull-request messages.\n- Never cite blank lines; double-check ranges.\n\nUpdating memories:\n\nYou can update the memories **only** when explicitly asked by the user. This must always come from a direct request from the user.\n- Write your update in /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n- Each update must be one small file containing what you want to add/delete/update from the memories.\n- The name of this file must be `<timestamp>-<short slug>.md`\n- Do not try to edit the memory files yourself, only add one update note in /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY BEGINS =========\nv1\n\n## User Profile\n\nThe user builds Stich, Keating, Twyne, Not Organic, dio.computer/dio-machines, Interleave, and Traction with Bun/TypeScript, Qwik/React, Convex, Railway, Nix/Home Manager, and agent tooling. They prefer source-backed autonomous work that preserves unrelated dirty changes and clearly separates deterministic checks from browser, provider, deployment, payment, registry, release, clean-install, and real-device proof. They want candid product evaluation against stated outcomes, not feature inventories. Product work should be distinctive, accessible, mobile-capable, customer/task-first, and grounded in real product states. Learner interactions should be immediate, compact, tactile, animated with purpose, and feel like games. Their architecture uses server-held credentials, private account/control planes, and provider-neutral execution. Account evolution, revisions, evidence, and authorization belong outside replaceable sandbox providers; Not Organic is the control-plane boundary. [ad-hoc note]\n\n## User preferences\n\n- Preserve unrelated dirty work; confirm scope before commits, deployments, version bumps, tags, releases, or destructive cleanup.\n- For filesystem cleanup, “don’t delete anything” is the default; leave non-git/local directories in place and delete only exact explicitly approved targets.\n- Never present unavailable verification as passing; name exact browser, provider, deployment, registry, payment, release, typecheck, and Vet boundaries.\n- For a product rating, assess the stated promise and outcomes; distinguish broad implementation from validated efficacy, especially when human-learner evidence is absent.\n- Once scope is clear, “just get straight to it.” When they explicitly say they will verify and say “skip vet,” implement directly without adding tests or running Vet.\n- When they explicitly say “Don't write tests right now” or “don't manual test things here ... pure implementation,” do not add tests or run test/browser checks; state the resulting verification boundary rather than quietly expanding scope.\n- Make learner-facing quizzes/flashcards “feel like games”: less text, tactile action, purposeful motion, selectable shader atmospheres, and an urgency timer that is visually primary.\n- For shared learner activities, use the canonical OpenUI/`learner-contracts` path across web, mobile, and TUI; timers are optional and “never speed-only scoring.”\n- For responsive controls, use icons only when space is narrow, then reveal labels at a larger breakpoint; preserve accessible names and avoid nested/double-boxed actionable rows.\n- For an OAuth/signup outage, trace the complete browser-to-token path. A local patch, container health, DNS/TLS, or CORS-looking error is not a working integration until a real exchange succeeds.\n- Chat sends should appear “the moment i press enter”: optimistically publish before credentials or preparation, then reconcile failures/acknowledgements.\n- A cross-platform desktop request includes installer, native dependencies, process/filesystem/OAuth/credential behavior, and a real GUI/runtime smoke test—not just a packaging target.\n- For Keating Linux distribution, retain AppImage, DEB, and RPM with an out-of-band public signing key; self-host a signed Flatpak repository unless the user asks for Flathub.\n- Prefer local-first learner submissions: save files/answers immediately, then distinguish “Saved on this device” from asynchronous “Delivered to course.”\n- Use native namespaced Devenv tasks and `git-hooks.nix`, not custom `.githooks`, root Just/Mise/Moon, or `concurrently`.\n- Use authentic, replaceable product captures in coherent spreads and realistic Storybook states; direct platform-aware downloads use real icons when artifacts exist.\n- Put consent/privacy in Settings rather than interrupting chat; use global default, partner override, and per-item approval.\n- Lead public work with customer-facing philosophy/outcomes and recognizable product states; separate investor and technical/developer content. For substantial reports, explain unfamiliar structured data through concrete trajectory examples and finish with evidence, limitations, next experiment, and what success means.\n- For Not Organic identity, build a first-party PDS signup path rather than routing people through Bluesky; retain `did:plc` unless a concrete need changes, and document WebVH tradeoffs instead of assuming a migration.\n- When authorized integrations exist and the user says “please just add these,” apply and verify the requested DNS/configuration changes; preserve unrelated records and keep SMTP/domain-delivery proof separate.\n- Secrets stay server-side and out of logs. A credential pasted in chat is compromised: revoke/replace it; store only names/metadata.\n- Keating releases use `bun pm version`, not Bumpy. Relationship-agent evaluation retains provenance, evidence/warrant/counterargument, safety, consent, uncertainty, and limits.\n- When t…5122 tokens truncated…only completion of remaining provider work: a675581, bun run check, 156 pass, SKIP=notorganic-railway, .zero/cache, No seeds found\n  - desc: Commit/push procedure that completed the dirty provider work while preserving generated cache and avoiding an unrelated Railway deploy.\n  - learnings: Exclude `.zero/cache/`; use `SKIP=notorganic-railway git push rad main` after reviewing hooks. Aligned refs prove acceptance, while “No seeds found” leaves peer replication unconfirmed.\n\n#### 2026-09-06\n\n- Product icons and private Radicle recovery: product-icons, 268e9bd, interleave-mark.png, twyne-mark.png, git push rad main, rad auth\n  - desc: Isolated Interleave/Twyne/Keating icon assets and the earlier signing/passphrase-blocked private-Radicle push in cwd=/home/diogenes/Projects/notorganic-provider.\n  - learnings: Preserve product-specific marks and dirty work; completion was subsequently verified at `a675581`, but private-peer replication remained unconfirmed.\n\n#### 2026-09-05\n\n- Public landing, private Radicle, portal, and emails: notorganic.info, build:landing, rad init, juice.js, preview:emails, Draft placeholder\n  - desc: Scoped Railway landing, legal/Paddle boundary, raster motion, portal, and React Email.\n  - learnings: Poll deployment success and keep API/docs separate; legal pages are drafts; transactional emails omit unsubscribe links.\n\n### /home/diogenes/Projects/dio-machines\n\n#### 2026-09-07\n\n- ChatGPT-style desktop GUI app support: gui.nix, APPIMAGES.md, desktop-apps, AppImage, workstation\n  - desc: Orientation for where workstation GUI applications belong in cwd=/home/diogenes/Projects/dio-machines; no app was selected or installed.\n  - learnings: `home/packages/gui.nix` is the Nix-managed workstation surface and `docs/APPIMAGES.md` covers outside-Nix apps; clarify the intended app/integration before editing.\n\n### /home/diogenes/Projects/traction\n\n#### 2026-09-05\n\n- Privacy-enforcing SDK and deployment preparation: @traction/sdk, DO_NOT_TRACK, POSTHOG_READ_KEY, pack:local, railway up --ci --serv