{"timestamp":"2026-09-20T06:50:50.121Z","ordinal":660,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0bd62-eabf-7a61-bb50-27a4047f7c4a","turn_id":"01a0bd69-84d5-7c33-89bf-e3198d5b775f","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_0d2613c88aa32939016aaf823d6df087d1a7392f6a28944582","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"Your concern was right: Twyne had the judgement tools, but too much depended on opening a panel and requesting them. I’ve connected them to the editor.\n\n- **Feedback beside your writing:** after a 650ms typing pause, Jev checks the current passage. A margin marker and compact status strip show findings without opening the board. Actual arrival time includes the provider response.\n- **Automatic broader review:** saved drafts trigger rubric, passage, brief, and evidence-supported writing-lens checks. Results appear progressively.\n- **Fast, bounded decisions:** narrow typed questions, cached answers, batched checks, request limits, and stale-result rejection. Pause/resume persists.\n- **Consistent presentation:** shared paper colours, typography, fields, buttons, and dropdowns, with mobile layout checks.\n\nAll five review findings are resolved: settings assertions, fictitious judge metrics, drag starting coordinates, mobile tour visibility, and circling focus. Browser testing also exposed—and helped fix—a selection popup blocking native drops.\n\nAgainst the TypeSafe approach and our recorded Keating work, this now delivers the missing editor integration: small judgements drive useful interface behaviour automatically. Keating’s recorded implementation was also partial; it isn’t proof that every intended decision path was complete.\n\nValidation: **1,470 tests passed, 39 skipped; five browser checks passed; typecheck, targeted lint, and client build passed.** Real hosted Jev latency and editorial usefulness remain unverified in this run.\n\n"}],"phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":{"entries":[{"path":"MEMORY.md","lineStart":32,"line